#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-campaign — the frame above the checkouts of one project: who holds
# which work package at the commit the remote stands at, read out of a fetched
# ref and never out of the working tree.
#
# THIS HEADER IS THE AUTHORITATIVE DOCUMENTATION of every sub-command, of the
# KEY=value output, of the state vocabulary and of the exit table. The row in
# README-hooks.md and every call site summarise it and do not restate it.
#
# ## Usage
#
#   fusion-campaign overview [--window <hours>] [--ref <upstream>]
#
# One sub-command at this version, and of it the reduced tier: the overview
# that needs no campaign record. The lane view, the rule checks and the
# steering sub-commands arrive with later versions; a sub-command this script
# does not know is a usage error, never a silent no-op.
#
#   --window <hours>  The overview window, a whole number of hours above zero,
#                     24 when absent. Nothing stores it: it is a parameter of
#                     the run and is printed back as `window_hours=`. No line
#                     of the reduced tier depends on it yet.
#   --ref <upstream>  The ref to read in place of the branch's upstream, e.g.
#                     `origin/main`. With it no branch is needed, so a detached
#                     HEAD and a branch that tracks nothing are both readable.
#                     Its remote is the longest configured remote name that
#                     prefixes it; a ref no remote prefixes is a local ref.
#
# ## `overview` — what it does, in order
#
# Resolve the workbench by walking up from pwd; require a git work tree;
# resolve the branch and its upstream (or take `--ref`); fetch that remote;
# name the commit the ref stands at; extract the tracked workbench at that
# commit into a scratch directory; read the extract through the codec; print.
#
# The fetch is `git fetch <remote>` with `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and a batch
# mode ssh, so a credential prompt is an immediate, reportable failure and a
# slow transfer stays visible and interruptible. The reasons for that shape,
# for the absence of a timeout and for fetching the whole remote are argued in
# `bin/fusion-forum` `## Why there is no timeout`, whose state vocabulary and
# exit codes this script takes over unchanged.
#
# What is extracted is the workbench manifest and setup marker, the container
# store, the campaign store and the event log, each as the commit holds it
# (`git archive` into a file, then `tar`); an entry in no tree at the commit is
# left out. The store names are never spelled here: the program reads them
# from `hooks/lib/stores.ts`, the TypeScript copy of the table `bin/fusion-stores`
# prints, which `hooks/lib/__tests__/fusion-stores.test.ts` holds equal to it.
#
# ## Output of `overview`, one KEY=value per line, in this order
#
#   state=ok            The only state that reaches exit 0. Every other value
#                       names why there is nothing to read against; see
#                       `## The state vocabulary`.
#   ref=<upstream>      The ref read: the upstream's short name, or the value
#                       of `--ref` as given.
#   head=<sha>          The commit that ref names AFTER the fetch. Everything
#                       below describes the tree of this commit.
#   head_time=<time>    That commit's committer time.
#   age_minutes=<n>     Whole minutes from `head_time` to now, never below 0.
#                       It is the age of the newest push to the ref, and so the
#                       least age of everything else on this page.
#   window_hours=<n>    The overview window of this run.
#   campaigns=<n>       The campaign files at the commit, as the codec's `list`
#                       names them. The reduced tier evaluates none: above 0, a
#                       `note=campaigns-not-evaluated` line says so.
#   held=<checkout>\t<alias|->\t<package>\t<last_push|none>\t<waits>
#                       One line per package whose control file reads `claimed`
#                       at the commit, TAB-separated, ascending by checkout,
#                       then by package. Zero lines is an answer: nobody holds
#                       anything at that commit.
#                         checkout   the eight hex characters the claim names
#                         alias      the registry's name for that checkout as it
#                                    stood at the commit
#                                    (`bin/fusion-checkout-name resolve <hex> --at
#                                    <head>`), `-` where the registry has no
#                                    entry or no alias for it
#                         package    the package's container name
#                         last_push  see `## last_push`; `none` where the
#                                    checkout's rows name no commit on the ref
#                         waits      `none`, or a comma-separated list, edges
#                                    first: `edge:<container>` per dependency
#                                    that blocks the package (an edge on a live
#                                    package, or an unmet condition on one that
#                                    is not live), then `decision:<basename>`
#                                    per open decision record inside the
#                                    package's container, named by its
#                                    narrative's basename
#   note=<token>[\t<field>...]
#                       Zero or more. A degradation that changed the answer,
#                       stated rather than hidden; see `## The notes`.
#
# Every time on this page is RFC 3339 in UTC at second resolution,
# `2026-11-09T13:04:44Z`.
#
# ## last_push
#
# The newest committer time among the commits on the ref that the checkout's
# `commit` rows in the event log name. Which commit a row names is decided
# over one listing of the head's ancestors, in this order: the one commit
# whose hash the row's short hash abbreviates; else the one commit that
# carries the row's subject and an author time within 60 seconds of the row's
# `ts`, which is what a rebase made of the row's commit, since a rebase keeps
# subject and author time and replaces the hash; else none, two candidates
# included. A row is never matched by guess.
#
# THE FIGURE IS A LOWER BOUND ON THE PUSH. A row that names no commit and is
# newer than the row the figure came from is counted in a
# `note=commit-rows-unmatched` line, so an under-report is never silent. And a
# row reaches the ref only with the commit that carries the log, so the figure
# can trail the checkout's newest push by that one commit. The measurement
# behind the rule, and the three alternatives it ruled out, are in
# `hooks/lib/campaign.ts`.
#
# ## The notes
#
#   commit-rows-unmatched\t<checkout>\t<count>\t<time>
#       `<count>` rows by that checkout name no commit on the ref and are newer
#       than the row its `last_push` came from; `<time>` is the newest such
#       row's `ts`. With `last_push` `none`, every row of the checkout that
#       names nothing is counted.
#   no-commit-rows\t<checkout>
#       The event log at the commit carries no `commit` row by that checkout,
#       so its `last_push` is `none` and no time was guessed.
#   event-log-untracked
#       The event log is in no tree at the commit; every `last_push` is `none`.
#   log-lines-unreadable\t<count>
#       That many lines of the event log are no JSON object, or are `commit`
#       rows without a `detail` or a readable `ts`. They were left out.
#   registry-unreadable\t<checkout>\t<exit code>
#       `bin/fusion-checkout-name` could not look the checkout up at the commit
#       (its exit code: 6 where the registry could not be read there, -1 where
#       the helper could not be started), so the alias column carries `-` for
#       a lookup that did not happen.
#   package-unreadable\t<package>\t<class>/<reason>
#       The codec reports that package's control file unreadable at the commit.
#       It is outside the read; a claim it carries prints no `held=` line.
#   campaigns-not-evaluated\t<count>
#       Campaign files stand at the commit and this version reads none.
#   local-ref
#       The ref belongs to no remote, so no fetch was owed and the answer is as
#       local as the ref is.
#
# ## Exit codes
#
#   0  the printed answer is the answer, `state=ok`; no `held=` line included.
#   1  the helper itself could not run: the compiled hooks or the codec bundle
#      are missing, `bin/fusion-tmp path` gave no scratch directory, `git` or
#      `tar` failed where no state names the failure, or an internal error.
#      The reason is on stderr and nothing is on stdout.
#   2  usage error: no sub-command or an unknown one, an unknown or repeated
#      flag, a flag without its value, `--window` that is no whole number above
#      zero, `--ref` that is empty or begins with a dash.
#   3  no fusion workbench above pwd.
#   4  the workbench at the commit was not read: the codec's gate refused it by
#      name (`legacy`, a workbench without its manifest at that commit, or
#      `unsupported`), or the codec refused a read or gave no answer. The cause
#      is on stderr and NOTHING is on stdout: a named failure and never an
#      empty overview.
#   5  nothing to read against: `state=` is `no-work-tree`, `no-branch`,
#      `no-upstream`, `upstream-unresolved` or `workbench-untracked`.
#   6  the fetch did not complete, `state=fetch-failed`. git's own stderr is
#      passed through unmodified.
#   7  the workbench is not inside this repository,
#      `state=workbench-outside-repo`.
#
# 2, 3, 5, 6 and 7 are `bin/fusion-forum`'s numbers with its meanings; 1 and 4
# are this script's own, that script having no codec to fail and no compiled
# half to miss.
#
# ## The state vocabulary
#
# The values and the order in which they are tested are those of
# `bin/fusion-forum` `## The state vocabulary`, which argues each.
#
#   ok                      Exit 0. The lines above are the answer.
#   no-work-tree            Exit 5. A workbench and no repository, so no remote
#                           and no ref.
#   no-branch               Exit 5. Detached HEAD and no `--ref`: a detached
#                           HEAD has no upstream, and none is guessed.
#   no-upstream             Exit 5. The branch tracks nothing and no `--ref`
#                           was given; `origin` is never assumed.
#   upstream-unresolved     Exit 5. The upstream, or the ref `--ref` names,
#                           resolves to no commit even after the fetch.
#   workbench-untracked     Exit 5. The workbench is in no tree at the commit:
#                           the project does not track it there, so nothing
#                           another checkout wrote can arrive by fetch, and an
#                           overview with no `held=` line would be a false
#                           answer.
#   fetch-failed            Exit 6.
#   workbench-outside-repo  Exit 7. The workbench marker sits in an ancestor of
#                           the repository, so the workbench has no path in a
#                           commit.
#
# On a state other than `ok` the `state=` line is followed by `branch=<name>`
# and `ref=<upstream>` as far as each was resolved before the read stopped,
# and by nothing else. With `--ref` no `branch=` line is printed.
#
# ## What it touches
#
# The fetch updates the repository's remote-tracking refs, as any fetch does.
# Nothing in the working tree is read except to find the workbench and the
# repository's root, and nothing in it is written: `git status --porcelain` is
# the same before and after. The one thing written is a scratch directory
# under what `bin/fusion-tmp path` prints, removed on every way out, an
# interrupt included. One side effect is that helper's and not this one's: on
# the first run in a checkout that has no identifier yet, the identity helper
# it calls mints one, as it does for every program that asks.
#
# It sends the codec no mutation. No cache, no index, no record: the overview
# is recomputed per run and discarded.
#
# The work is hooks/dist/campaign.js, resolved relative to this script rather
# than through $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, so an install copy and a work tree each run
# their own build, their own codec bundle and their own sibling helpers. node
# is already a hard requirement: the plugin's hooks are node programs.

set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
entry="$here/../hooks/dist/campaign.js"

if [ ! -f "$entry" ]; then
  echo "fusion-campaign: $entry is missing — the plugin's compiled hooks are not installed." >&2
  exit 1
fi

exec node "$entry" "$@"
